## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model DP with a multi‑dimensional state and non‑trivial transitions, mixing path constraints with exact move counts. It also probes memoization vs bottom‑up, boundary handling, and memory optimizations essential for large grids.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) representing position and moves
- Transitions from (i-1,j,t-1), (i,j-1,t-1), (i-1,j-1,t-1)
- -INF for invalid states; base cases
- Memory optimization via rolling arrays
- Trade-offs between readability (memoized DFS) and performance (tabulation)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridSum(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array.from({length:m}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  function dfs(i,j,t){
    if(i<0||j<0||t<0) return NEG;
    if(i===0&&j===0&&t===0) return grid[0][0];
    if(memo[i][j][t]!==NEG) return memo[i][j][t];
    const best = Math.max(dfs(i-1,j,t-1), dfs(i,j-1,t-1), dfs(i-1,j-1,t-1));
    memo[i][j][t] = (best===NEG)? NEG : grid[i][j] + best;
    return memo[i][j][t];
  }
  return dfs(n-1,m-1,k);
}
```

